\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The results establish three insights that frame CIVO as a structural vulnerability of flat temporal conditioning in imagination-based RL. Full mechanistic analysis, gradient decomposition, and seed-divergence discussion are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}.

\paragraph{CIVO decomposition.}
The 008d configuration suffers 86\% degradation from baseline; full-pathway SGS (exp\_018) reduces this to 51\%.  The 35-percentage-point difference isolates CIVO's independent contribution from the inherent cost of $\dt{=}2$ imagination.  The RSSM reward head develops a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), providing the systematic bias that the critic internalizes and the actor exploits---a four-stage feedback loop analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced rather than policy-class-induced.

\paragraph{Gradient evolution and intervention design.}
\label{sec:gradient_evolution_main}
Gradient decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:gradient_decomposition}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}) reveals that the composition of $\nabla_\theta e_{\Delta t}$ evolves substantially during training: at 55K steps (CIVO onset), KL dynamics contributes 50.7\%, observation decoders 25.8\%, and the reward head 23.5\%; by convergence (95K), observation decoders dominate at 92.7\% while the reward head diminishes to 0.0\%.  This explains why reward-only SGS (exp\_016e) is functionally insufficient---it targets a pathway whose contribution vanishes---while full-pathway SGS, which removes the dominant observation decoder gradient, \sgsclaim{} (peak NVE $\sgsnve{}\times$, eval \sgseval{}).  Transient threshold exceedances from the residual 7\% KL pathway indicate that observation decoder gradients are the primary but not sole CIVO driver.

\paragraph{Architectural contrast with MPC.}
$\dt$-conditioning succeeds in MPC \citep{nhu2025tawm} because re-planning from real states prevents error accumulation through the critic.  In imagination-based training, the closed actor-critic loop amplifies any systematic reward-prediction bias---the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics.  Multiple vulnerability pathways exist: CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and non-CIVO instability (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing, \S\ref{sec:f6}), suggesting that mitigation strategies targeting the imagination channel alone may be insufficient.

