\end{table}

At convergence (95K steps), the reward head's gradient contribution to the temporal embedding has diminished to negligible levels---zero to within floating-point precision. Exp\_016e's stop-gradient intervention detaches a gradient pathway that, by this stage, carries no signal---it is functionally insufficient for CIVO prevention.

The gradient composition evolves substantially during training: at 55K steps (CIVO onset), the distribution is KL 50.7\%, observation decoders 25.8\%, and reward head 23.5\%; by convergence (95K steps), observation decoders dominate at 92.7\% while the reward head diminishes to 0.0\%. This shift reflects the relative output dimensionality: the observation decoders reconstruct a 24-dimensional proprioceptive signal (14 orientations, 9 velocities, 1 height), while the reward head predicts a single scalar. As training progresses, this ${\sim}24\!\times$ dimensionality asymmetry, compounded by DreamerV3's symlog reward scaling, causes the observation decoder pathway to increasingly dominate temporal embedding learning.

\subsubsection{Implications for the CIVO Mechanism}
\label{sec:sgs_implications}

The gradient decomposition reframes the mechanistic understanding of how the temporal embedding drives CIVO.

\paragraph{Observation reconstruction dominates temporal embedding learning at convergence.} By 95K steps, the temporal embedding's parameters are shaped almost entirely (92.7\%) by the observation reconstruction objective. This means $e_{\Delta t}$ ultimately learns to encode temporal information that helps minimize \textit{observation prediction error}, not reward prediction error. If this learned representation introduces systematic biases in latent state predictions at $\Delta t{>}1$---for instance, by encouraging the RSSM to produce states that are easy to decode but dynamically inaccurate---these biases propagate to \textit{all} downstream computation, including reward prediction and the critic's value estimates. CIVO thus originates in the observation reconstruction objective's influence on latent dynamics, not in reward-specific pathways.

\paragraph{Reframing the intervention target.} At convergence, any gradient-level strategy that targets the reward head alone---reward prediction regularization, reward-conditioned value clipping, or the reward-only SGS tested here---addresses $<$1\% of the gradient flow to $e_{\Delta t}$ and is functionally insufficient for CIVO prevention. Effective gradient-level interventions must address the dominant observation decoder pathway. This motivates the \textit{full-pathway SGS} design (exp\_018): detaching $e_{\Delta t}$ from all decoder and reward gradients, retaining only the KL dynamics contribution (7.3\% at convergence).

\paragraph{Connection to RSSM fidelity analysis.} The observation-dominated gradient structure at convergence is consistent with the RSSM fidelity results in Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}: the trained-embedding condition (exp\_008d) shows a $2.78\times$ reward gap between $\Delta t{=}1$ and $\Delta t{=}2$ transitions, despite the reward head's gradient contribution diminishing to negligible levels by convergence. This confirms that the reward head's $\Delta t$-dependent biases are inherited \textit{through the latent state}---which is shaped by observation reconstruction gradients---rather than learned directly from the reward objective.

\subsubsection{Exp\_016e Reinterpreted as Stochastic Variation}
\label{sec:016e_reinterp}

With the gradient decomposition establishing that exp\_016e is functionally insufficient (targeting a pathway that carries $<$1\% of gradient flow at convergence), the apparent timing differences between exp\_016e and exp\_008d---later collapse onset (75K vs.\ 25K) and higher peak evaluation (326.7 vs.\ 167.2)---must be attributed to stochastic variation rather than SGS effects. All three trajectories (exp\_008d, exp\_009b, exp\_016e) share the invariant identified by the NVE diagnostic: each crosses the $1.5\times$ pathological threshold at some point during training.

\subsection{Three Post-CIVO Outcomes: Why Seeds Diverge}
\label{sec:seed_divergence}

The three post-CIVO outcomes (Table~\ref{tab:post_civo})---full collapse, transient CIVO, and oscillatory degradation---all cross the $\nve > 1.5\times$ threshold, yet the downstream damage is stochastic.  We hypothesize that the divergence relates to the critic's representational capacity at the time of CIVO onset.  \citet{lyle2022capacity} demonstrate that deep RL networks suffer progressive capacity loss, in which early-learned features become increasingly difficult to overwrite.  If the critic's capacity is already partially consumed when CIVO injects overestimation, the network cannot recalibrate (seed~0); if sufficient capacity remains, the critic can absorb transient overestimation and recover (seed~2).

\subsection{Why MPC Is Immune}
\label{sec:mpc_immune}

\citet{nhu2025tawm} show that $\dt$-conditioning benefits TD-MPC2, raising the question of why it fails in DreamerV3.  The critical difference is that MPC re-plans from real states at every step---imagination errors do not accumulate into the training signal because there is no critic bootstrapping from imagined trajectories.  In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination, and any systematic reward-prediction bias at $\dt > 1$ is amplified through the closed actor-critic loop.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$\dt$}{dt}-Embedding Dilemma}
\label{sec:embedding_dilemma}

The factorial results reveal a fundamental tension: removing the $\dt$-embedding (008f) eliminates CIVO but at a $13\times$ cost to reward prediction quality ($0.7469$ vs.\ $0.0568$ MAE)---the world model cannot distinguish temporal scales.  Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding (008e\_v3) prevents CIVO in 1 of 2 seeds but the other collapses via Pathway~B (\S\ref{sec:f6}).  Neither approach alone provides a complete solution.

\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}
\label{sec:pathways}

The two collapse mechanisms identified in \S\ref{sec:f6}---CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and non-CIVO instability (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing)---have distinct diagnostic signatures but a shared root cause: the trained $\dt$-embedding's learned features affect both reward prediction across temporal scales (Pathway~A) and the representation space in which the actor operates (Pathway~B).  This explains why $\nve$ is sensitive but not perfectly specific: Pathway~B's late evaluation decline inflates $\nve$ through the same arithmetic as CIVO, yielding the sole false positive ($3.78\times$ for 008e\_v3 s2).  Distinguishing the two requires examining the temporal profile and entropy direction of collapse.

\subsection{Implications for Temporal Abstraction in World Models}
\label{sec:implications}

CIVO identifies a structural incompatibility between flat temporal conditioning and imagination-based actor-critic training.  Hierarchical approaches---THICK \citep{gumbsch2024thick}, Hieros \citep{mattes2024hieros}, LS-Imagine \citep{li2025lsimagine}---may avoid this by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale.  Whether CIVO transfers to transformer-based world models (DreamerV4; \citealt{hafner2025dreamerv4}) depends on whether attention weights co-adapt with temporal embeddings analogously.

